<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04WC">Remark <span data-tag="04WC">7.6.2.26</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category which admits fiber products. Then, for every object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the slice $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/X}$ admits finite products (Corollary <a data-tag="03FV" href="/tag/03FV">7.6.2.24</a>). Let $\operatorname{Sub}(X)$ denote the collection of isomorphism classes of subobjects of $X$ (Notation <a data-tag="04W9" href="/tag/04W9">4.7.5.20</a>). Then $\operatorname{Sub}(X)$ is a lower semilattice: that is, it has a largest element (given by the isomorphism class $[X]$) and every pair of objects $[X_0], [X_1] \in \operatorname{Sub}(X)$ has a greatest lower bound $[X_0] \cap [X_1]$ in $\operatorname{Sub}(X)$, given concretely by the isomorphism class of the fiber product $[X_0 \times _{X} X_1]$. </p>
